rikes that NATO are  committing are not being done without  any kind of liaison with rebels on the  ground  because the danger is if that happens it  is the Assad regime that had most to  gain from them if there's one thing the  Turks understand it is that threats and  dangers posed by their neighbors this  has always been something that they've  been deeply preoccupied by they have a  number of surveillance aircraft that  have been flying along the border areas  with Syria occasionally conducting  incursions into Syria they will have  access of course to all of NATO's aerial  surveillance that's been flown now for  many months over Syrian territory and of  course they have state-of-the-art  fighter bombers that can strike deep  inside Syria on top of that they've also  got surface-to-surface missiles which  bring a great deal more firepower and  arguably more accuracy to a campaign so  that they can actually use these to wipe  out very significant wide areas of  concentration of Islamic state forces so  all of that combined means that the  Turks have a great deal to contribute  and then at the back of both the minds  of people in the capital in both  countries would be whether or not the  Turks are prepared to send in ground  troops now they haven't ruled that out  which means that they may well be  expected to conduct ground incursion x'  to seal off a particular important  communications route and above all to  protect those vulnerable populations
 

